  "goodToKnow": "Der Premierminister (die Premierministerin) is a prime minister, used for countries such as the UK, Canada or India. Germany's head of government is der Bundeskanzler instead, and the heads of the German Länder are called Ministerpräsident. For some countries, such as Israel or Italy, German media often say Ministerpräsident too. The first part is French, so it's pronounced pre-MYAY. The plural stays the same."
